## Who is making them

Google names five partners: Acer, ASUS, Dell, HP and Lenovo. The article says it is working with them to make the first Googlebooks. It does not list any model names, processors, memory, screen sizes or battery life, so any spec sheet you see elsewhere did not come from this announcement.

## What Google has not said

The article does not give a price, a release date beyond "this fall", or a list of countries. It also does not say which Gemini features need a paid Google plan, or whether the AI features run on the device or in the cloud. Those are the questions that decide whether a Googlebook is worth buying for a small business, and they are unanswered in the source used here.

Fall 2026 has now largely passed. In this run the only other Google page I could read was a category page whose article list did not load, so I could not confirm that any Googlebook is on sale. The article points readers to googlebook.com for updates, which I did not read. If you are thinking of buying one, check that site and the retailers' own listings for price, specs and return terms.

## How to think about it for work

If you already use Android and Google Workspace, the pitch is fewer steps between phone and laptop and an assistant that sees your mail and calendar. That also means giving a Gemini feature access to that data, so read the privacy settings and your workspace admin's policy before turning on anything that reaches into Gmail or Calendar. Treat the demos in the announcement as Google's description of intent, not as measured results.

This is general information about a product announcement, not financial advice.
